<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jahanara Begum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1614">1614</a>–1681</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Padshah Begum, Sahibat-uz-Zamani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jahanara Begum was a Mughal princess, the eldest surviving child of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> and Mumtaz Mahal. After her mother's death in 1631, the 17-year-old was given the royal seal and the title Padshah Begum, first lady of the empire, and she wielded great political influence in her father's reign. In the war of succession after Shah Jahan's illness in 1657 she backed her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dara-shikoh">Dara Shikoh</a>, then cared for her father under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s house arrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> Fort until his death in 1666, later reconciling with Aurangzeb. She wrote a biography of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint Mu'in al-Din Chishti, funded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a> Masjid of Agra and is buried at the Nizamuddin Dargah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jahanara-begum.jpg" alt="Jahanara Begum" width="330" height="564" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: La'lchand · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Princess_Jahanara_aged_18,_British_Library,_Add_Or_3129,_f.13v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jahanara Begum was born on 23 March 1614, the second and eldest surviving child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> and Mumtaz Mahal. Her first teacher was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">Sati</a> al-Nisa Khanam, sister of the poet laureate Talib Amuli, who was known for her knowledge of the Qur'an, Persian literature and medicine.</p><p>When Mumtaz Mahal died in 1631, Jahanara, aged 17, took her place as first lady of the empire, though her father had three other wives. She was given charge of the imperial seal, with the right to issue farmans and nishans, and was granted the title Padshah Begum. She lived in her own palace outside Agra Fort, where she held a court, and nobles and foreign ambassadors sought her intervention with the emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In March 1644 Jahanara suffered serious burns, and accounts differ on how: either her perfumed garments caught fire, or she was burned while helping a dancing woman whose dress had ignited. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> ordered alms for the poor and the release of prisoners, and she recovered after about a year. She then received the revenues of the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a>. She owned ships and kept up trade with the English and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> fell seriously ill in 1657, war broke out among his four sons. Jahanara backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dara-shikoh">Dara Shikoh</a>, the eldest. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> defeated Dara at Samugarh on 29 May 1658, she proposed a partition of the empire, which Aurangzeb refused. Aurangzeb besieged Agra Fort and held his father prisoner there, and Jahanara joined him and cared for him until his death in 1666. After this she reconciled with Aurangzeb, who restored her titles and made her first lady of the court again. She is recorded as arguing against his austerity measures and his restoration of the poll tax on non-Muslims in 1679.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dara-shikoh">Dara Shikoh</a> she became a disciple of Mullah Shah Badakhshi, who initiated her into the Qadiriyya Sufi order in 1641. She wrote Mu'nis al-Arwāḥ, a biography of Moinuddin Chishti, the founder of the Chishti Order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, whom she regarded as her spiritual guide, and a biography of Mullah Shah. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> she paid for the Jami Masjid, built in 1648, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> attached to it. She died in 1681 and was buried in the Nizamuddin Dargah complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in a marble tomb she had built during her lifetime, open to the sky and covered only with grass.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jahanara Begum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.18,78.02&amp;year=1657" aria-label="Agra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6602 1167 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jahanara Begum"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7052 1437h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7052 1437h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.18,78.02&amp;year=1657">What was here in 1657? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
